          "parameters": {
            "template": {
              "kind": "text",
              "binding": {
                "text": "Write the guide and the reference for this feature.\n\n{{.inputs.standard}}\n\n## The feature\n\n{{.inputs.feature}}\n\n## The units it is built from\n\n{{.inputs.units}}\n\n## The docs the build touched\n\n{{.inputs.paths}}\n\n## What came back\n\n{{.inputs.returned}}\n\n## What to write\n\nA guide is for a person mid-task with a question. Its first ten lines get them unstuck. It describes what exists, checkable against the code that shipped. Its example runs from the state a new reader is in. Where a guide already covers the task, edit that guide.\n\nA reference entry exists for every contract an outside person can reach.\n\nOpen `docs/_templates/guide.md` and follow its headings.\n\n## What to produce\n\n`guides`: the guide docs, complete files.\n\n`reference`: one entry per public contract: `{ id, path, summary }`.\n"
              }
            },
            "values": {
              "kind": "json",
              "binding": {
                "op": {
                  "kind": "function",
                  "functionRef": "op.record",
                  "input": {
                    "standard": {
                      "kind": "text",
                      "binding": {
                        "text": "## How a doc is written\n\nA doc is a standalone source of truth. Someone who has never seen this run, this conversation, or any earlier draft reads it and knows what is true.\n\n- **Only what is true.** Every sentence states a fact about the product, the design or the code. Nothing is hedged, argued or defended.\n- **As little as achieves the goal.** Keep a detail only if a reader would otherwise build the wrong thing, pick the wrong pattern, or miss something they need. Cut everything else.\n- **Headings carry the idea.** Every heading is a sentence that states its conclusion. Reading the headings alone gives the whole doc; the text under a heading holds only the detail a builder needs.\n- **One concept per file.** One feature, one pattern, one component, one unit. A doc that describes two things is two docs.\n- **Layers reference upward only.** Product names people, goals and what they can do. UX names patterns and product features. UI names components, patterns and features. Engineering names units, components, patterns and features. No layer names anything from a layer below it: a product doc never names a class, a file, a table, a column or a component.\n- **Each layer writes at its own altitude.** Product describes outcomes: what a person can now do and why it matters, never how. A list, an order, a row, a click, a panel or a screen in a product doc is a defect. UX describes interactions: the steps, the states, what the person knows at each. It never describes visuals. UI describes visuals: hierarchy, copy, states drawn. It never describes code. Engineering describes code.\n- **No questions.** A doc holds answers. What cannot be settled from the docs is asked of the person now, through `questions`, and the answer is written in as a plain statement.\n- **No alternatives, unless the choice goes against the usual way.** A doc does not list what was considered. Where it departs from the normal way of doing things, it states the departure and the reason in one line.\n- **No process.** A doc never mentions a draft, a pass, a revision, a critique, a finding, a conversation, an earlier version of itself, or anything \"previously\" said. Where a decision changed, the doc states the decision that holds.\n- **Frontmatter is the record.** The fields the template declares carry the links between layers. They are complete and they resolve.\n\nWrite in plain sentences. No em-dashes, no parentheses, no rhetorical questions.\n"
                      }
                    },
